  data-title='Case Study: Luma Stays — Web &amp; Interaction Design | Katja Turnsek'
  data-description='UX/UI case study for an accommodation platform, focused on search, booking decisions, responsive layouts, and distinct customer and venue-manager flows.'
  data-case='lumastays'
  aria-labelledby='case-lumastays-design-title'
>
  <div class='rows'>
    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-header'>
        <p class='case-eyebrow'>Web &amp; Interaction Design</p>
        <h1 id='case-lumastays-design-title'>Luma Stays</h1>
        <p class='case-subtitle'>
          A calm accommodation platform designed around confident booking and practical venue
          management.
        </p>

        <dl class='case-summary' aria-label='Project summary'>
          <div>
            <dt>Role</dt>
            <dd>UX/UI design and prototyping</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Scope</dt>
            <dd>Individual Project Exam 2</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Year</dt>
            <dd>2026</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Tools</dt>
            <dd>Figma, responsive UI, prototyping, design system</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Outcome</dt>
            <dd>Grade A project</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>

        <div class='tech-demo-wrapper case-demo-wrapper'>
          <div class='demo-links' data-auto='demo' data-case='lumastays-design'></div>
        </div>

        <nav
          class='project-switcher'
          aria-label='Luma Stays sections'
          data-auto='switcher'
          data-case='lumastays'
        ></nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>The brief</h2>
      <p>
        Luma Stays needed to support two connected but very different journeys. Customers had to
        search a large catalogue, compare venues, check availability, and complete a booking. Venue
        managers needed to create listings, maintain venue information, and review bookings. I
        designed both sides as parts of the same product, using shared patterns where they made
        sense without forcing different tasks into identical layouts.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-grid'>
        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--challenge'>
          <h2>Challenge</h2>
          <p>
            Choosing accommodation involves several decisions at once. Price, location, capacity,
            facilities, images, and availability all matter, but presenting everything with equal
            weight would make the interface difficult to scan. The design needed to reveal enough
            information for each decision while keeping search and booking straightforward.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Make venue cards useful for comparison without overloading them
            </li>
            <li>
              Keep search and filters connected to the results they change
            </li>
            <li>
              Give booking controls enough emphasis without pushing venue information aside
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--explore'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/design-lumastays-explore-600.webp'
            data-src='design-lumastays-explore-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='411'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Luma Stays explore design showing search, filters, and accommodation cards'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>
            Search and filtering remain connected to the venue grid
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--decisions'>
          <h2>Key decisions</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              I limited venue cards to the details needed for an initial comparison: image, title,
              location, guest capacity, rating, and price. The full venue page carries the
              information needed for a final decision.
            </li>
            <li>
              Search and guest filtering sit close to the results so users can see what changed. The
              controls remain easy to adjust instead of disappearing once the catalogue updates.
            </li>
            <li>
              On the venue page, I separated descriptive content, facilities, practical details, and
              booking controls into distinct areas. This allows users to evaluate the stay without
              losing sight of the next action.
            </li>
            <li>
              I used a restrained colour palette, generous spacing, and repeated card and form
              patterns to give a content-heavy product a quieter rhythm. Responsive layouts were
              designed around task priority rather than simply stacking every desktop element.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--booking'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/design-lumastays-booking-flow-600.webp'
            data-src='design-lumastays-booking-flow-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='411'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Luma Stays venue detail design showing information, guest selection, and booking controls'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>
            Venue information and booking controls share one clear decision flow
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class='case-study-block'>
          <h2>Role-based flow</h2>
          <p>
            Customers and venue managers use the same authentication, navigation, and profile
            foundation, but their priorities are not the same. Customer profiles focus on planned
            stays and booking history. Managers need immediate access to the venues they own and the
            actions required to maintain them. I kept the shared structure familiar while changing
            the content hierarchy and available controls according to the user’s role.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Customer profiles place upcoming and previous bookings first
            </li>
            <li>
              Manager profiles surface owned venues and listing-management actions
            </li>
            <li>
              Editing controls appear when ownership makes them relevant, keeping the public
              experience uncluttered
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/design-lumastays-manager-dashboard-600.webp'
            data-src='design-lumastays-manager-dashboard-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='401'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Luma Stays manager dashboard design showing profile information and managed venues'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>
            Manager dashboard with owned venues and account context
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row case-outcome'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>Outcome and reflection</h2>
      <p>
        The final design connects discovery, venue comparison, booking, profiles, and listing
        management without treating them as isolated features. The completed application received a
        <strong>Grade A</strong>. The most valuable part of the project was designing one system for
        users with different permissions and goals. It pushed me to think beyond individual screens
        and consider what information should persist, what should change with the user’s role, and
        when an action needs to become visible.
      </p>
    </div>

